    by inequalities (\ref{general_derivate_by_y_not_in_S}), (\ref{general_derivate_by_y_in_S}), and the fact that $\bar{y}_{ij}\leq 1$. 

To summarize, combining~(\ref{first_order_condition_in_x}) and (\ref{first_order_condition_in_y}) we can conclude that
    \begin{align*}
        \nabla h_G(x^*,y^*)^\top d = \nabla_xh_G(x^*,y^*)^\top d_x + \nabla_yh_G(x^*,y^*)^\top d_y \leq 0.
    \end{align*}
    This finally proves that first-order condition (\ref{general_second_order_sufficient_condition_1}) is satisfied.  


Now, in order to show that the second condition
(\ref{general_second_order_sufficient_condition_2}) is also satisfied,
notice that both terms in
\[
\nabla h_G(x^*,y^*)^\top d
=
\nabla_xh_G(x^*,y^*)^\top d_x
+
\nabla_yh_G(x^*,y^*)^\top d_y
\]
are nonpositive by (\ref{first_order_condition_in_x}) and
(\ref{first_order_condition_in_y}). Therefore, equality can hold only if
both terms vanish. By the strict part of Lemma
\ref{negative_scalar_product_with_the_penalty_term} and the strict
inequalities
(\ref{general_derivate_by_y_not_in_S}) and
(\ref{general_derivate_by_y_in_S}), this requires
$(\bar{x},\bar{y})$ to satisfy
\begin{itemize}
    \par\hangindent 40\p@ $\operatorname{supp}(\bar{x})\subseteq C$;
    \par\hangindent 40\p@ $\bar{y}_{ij}=1$ for every $\{i,j\}\in S$, while
    $\bar{y}_{lk}=0$ for every $\{l,k\}\in\bar{S}$.
\end{itemize}
In particular, the second condition is equivalent to $\bar{y}=y^*$.






We now show that these conditions are also sufficient for equality. Indeed, if $\operatorname{supp}(\bar{x})\subseteq C$, then $d_x=\bar{x}-x^*$ is supported on $C$ and \[ e^\top d_C = e^\top\bar{x}_C-e^\top x_C^* = 1-1=0. \] Since $C$ is a clique in the auxiliary graph $G^*$, the components of the vector $A_{G^*}x(C)$ indexed by $C$ are all equal to $\frac{|C|-1}{|C|}$. Moreover, condition \ref{cond_C3} implies that the components of $\nabla\Phi(x(C))$ indexed by $C$ are equal, since $x(C)$ is unchanged by every permutation of the vertices within $C$. Therefore, there exists $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $ \nabla_C\Phi(x(C))=\lambda e, $ and hence \begin{align*} \nabla_xh_G(x^*,y^*)^\top d_x &= 2x(C)^\top A_{G^*}d_x +\nabla\Phi(x(C))^\top d_x\\ &= \left( 2\frac{|C|-1}{|C|}+\lambda \right)e^\top d_C =0. \end{align*} Furthermore, $\bar{y}=y^*$ implies $d_y=\underline{0}$ and therefore $ \nabla_yh_G(x^*,y^*)^\top d_y=0. $

Thus, the two conditions above are also sufficient for equality, and we obtain \begin{align*} T^0((x^*,y^*),\nabla h_G(x^*,y^*)) &= \left\{ d\in T(x^*,y^*) \ \middle|\ d=(\bar{x},\bar{y})-(x^*,y^*),\ \bar{x}_{\bar C}=\underline{0},\ \bar{y}=y^* \right\}\\ &= \left\{ d=(d_x,d_y)\in T(x^*,y^*) \ \middle|\ d_{\bar C}=\underline{0},\ d_y=\underline{0} \right\}. \end{align*}






For any $U\subseteq V$, we denote by $\nabla^2_{UU}h_G(x^*,y^*)$ the principal submatrix of $\nabla^2h_G(x^*,y^*)$ corresponding to the $x$-variables indexed by $U$. In particular, 

    for any distinct pair of vertices $i,j\in C$, we have \begin{align*} \nabla^2_{x_ix_j}h_G(x^*,y^*) = 2+\nabla^2_{x_ix_j}\Phi(x^*) \end{align*} since $C$ is a clique in the auxiliary graph $G^*$, while \begin{align*} \nabla^2_{x_ix_i}h_G(x^*,y^*) = \nabla^2_{x_ix_i}\Phi(x^*) \end{align*} for every $i\in C$. Therefore, \begin{align*} \nabla^2_{CC}h_G(x^*,y^*) = 2ee^\top-2\mathbb{I}_{|C|} +\nabla^2_{CC}\Phi(x^*). \end{align*}












Finally, let \[ d\in T^0((x^*,y^*),\nabla h_G(x^*,y^*))\setminus\{0\}. \] By the preceding characterization, $d_y=\underline{0}$ and $d_{\bar C}=\underline{0}$. Hence, only the components of $d_x$ indexed by $C$ contribute to the quadratic form, and we have $ e^\top d_C=0 $ because $d_x$ is a feasible direction for the standard simplex. Consequently, \begin{align*} d^\top\nabla^2h_G(x^*,y^*)d &= d_C^\top\nabla^2_{CC}h_G(x^*,y^*)d_C\\ &= d_C^\top \left( 2ee^\top-2\mathbb{I}_{|C|} +\nabla^2_{CC}\Phi(x^*) \right)d_C\\ &= d_C^\top \left( \nabla^2_{CC}\Phi(x^*) -2\mathbb{I}_{|C|} \right)d_C<0. \end{align*} The strict inequality follows from condition \ref{cond_C2}, since every principal submatrix of a negative definite matrix is negative definite. This proves that the second condition (\ref{general_second_order_sufficient_condition_2}) is satisfied.













    To summarize, we have proved that the given point $(x^*,y^*)$ satisfies the second-order sufficient optimality conditions (\ref{general_second_order_sufficient_condition_1}) and (\ref{general_second_order_sufficient_condition_2}) for the considered regularized formulation (\ref{general_regularized_formulation_MsPP}) and hence it is a strict local maximizer.\\

    \fbox{$(ii)\Longrightarrow(i)$} is trivial.

