Let $(x^*,y^*)$ be a global maximizer of (\ref{general_regularized_formulation_MsPP}). By Theorem~\ref{general_local_equiv_prop} we have that $y^*$ is binary, $x^*=x(C)$ for an $s$-plex $C$  in $G$ and $y^*_{ij}=1$ for every $\{i,j\}\in\bar{E}(C)$. Moreover, the corresponding value of the objective function is 
    \begin{align*}
        h_G(x^*,y^*) = 1 - \frac{1}{|C|} + \Phi(x(C)).
    \end{align*}
    Assume by contradiction that there exists an $s$-plex $C^1\subseteq V$  in $G$ such that $|C^1|>|C|$. By the heredity property of the $s$-plex model (see \cite{clique_relaxations} and \cite{heredity} for further details) there exists an $s$-plex $C^2\subset C^1$ such that $|C|=|C^2|$. Therefore, let $(x^1,y^1)$ be the solution corresponding to the $s$-plex $C^1$, where $x^1=x(C^1)$ and $y^1_{ij}=1$ if $\{i,j\}\in\bar{E}(C^1)$ and $0$ otherwise, while $(x^2,y^2)=(x(C^2),y^1)$ is a feasible solution of (\ref{general_regularized_formulation_MsPP}) corresponding to $C^2$. With these choices, we can compute
    \begin{align*}
        h_G(x^*,y^*) = 1-\frac{1}{|C|}+\Phi(x(C)) = 1-\frac{1}{|C^2|}+\Phi(x(C^2))=h_G(x^2,y^2)
    \end{align*}
    since $\Phi$ satisfies \ref{cond_C3} and $x(C^2)\in\mathcal{P}(x(C))$. Moreover, if we now fix the $y$ component of problem~(\ref{general_regularized_formulation_MsPP}) as $y\equiv y^1$, then $(x^1,y^1)$ and $(x^2,y^2)$ are both feasible solutions for the regularized version of the MS for the MCP over the auxiliary graph $G^1$ induced by $y^1$, so
    \begin{align*}
    \begin{split}
        \max \quad & k_{G^1}(x) \\
        s.t. \quad & x\in\Delta_n
    \end{split}
    \end{align*}
    where the objective function is given by $k_{G^1}(x):=x^\top A_{G^1}x\ +\ \Phi(x)$. Finally, by Lemma \ref{increasing_value_result}, since $|C^2|<|C^1|$ by construction, we get
    \begin{align*}
        k_{G^1}(x^2)<k_{G^1}(x^1) &\Longleftrightarrow h_G(x^2,y^1)<h_G(x^1,y^1) \\
        &\Longleftrightarrow h_G(x^2,y^2)<h_G(x^1,y^1) \\ 
        &\Longleftrightarrow h_G(x^*,y^*)<h_G(x^1,y^1)
    \end{align*}
    leading to a contradiction with the global optimality of $(x^*,y^*)$.\\
    The converse follows immediately from the fact that any vector $(x^*,y^*)\in\Delta_n\times P'_s(G)$ satisfying the properties outlined in the above statement has the same value of the objective function $h_G$ as any global maximizer of (\ref{general_regularized_formulation_MsPP}).

